WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.560
A whale looks more like a tuna than a hippo.

2
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:06.220
So why does the family tree pair whale with hippo?

3
00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:11.080
Shape offers one clue, but ancestry can leave a different pattern.

4
00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.960
Watch which features travel together through the branches.

5
00:00:18.617 --> 00:00:20.877
Whales and tuna both move through water.

6
00:00:22.177 --> 00:00:25.077
A smooth body and fins help both animals swim.

7
00:00:26.797 --> 00:00:31.697
Similar conditions can favour similar solutions, even in groups that are not close

8
00:00:31.697 --> 00:00:35.737
relatives. Scientists therefore ask a sharper question.

9
00:00:37.057 --> 00:00:39.857
Which similarities were inherited from a shared ancestor?

10
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.320
Suppose you could follow only one trail.

11
00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:48.140
Would you choose the outside shape, or features passed through generations?

12
00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:54.960
Hold your answer. The useful trail should explain several different observations,

13
00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:56.740
not just one resemblance.

14
00:01:02.917 --> 00:01:04.557
Start inside the front limb.

15
00:01:05.797 --> 00:01:09.417
A human hand, bat wing, and whale flipper do different jobs.

16
00:01:10.657 --> 00:01:12.977
Yet each keeps the same basic bone pattern.

17
00:01:14.457 --> 00:01:17.737
Matching parts shift into new shapes without losing their order.

18
00:01:18.977 --> 00:01:23.277
That pattern is a homologous structure, inherited from a common ancestor.

19
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.120
Development and fossils add other views.

20
00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:32.620
Vertebrate embryos can begin with shared parts, then follow different paths.

21
00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.680
Fossils place past body forms in time order.

22
00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:42.240
Whale embryos begin hind limbs, and fossil whales had working legs.

23
00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:47.020
Neither clue depends on an adult whale looking like a fish.

24
00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:50.593
DNA supplies another record.

25
00:01:52.333 --> 00:01:56.733
Scientists compare corresponding sequences and look for changes inherited together.

26
00:01:58.293 --> 00:02:02.093
Shared insertions at matching locations are especially useful clues.

27
00:02:03.613 --> 00:02:05.993
Several such clues place whales beside hippos.

28
00:02:07.233 --> 00:02:12.393
When body, embryo, fossil, and DNA evidence agree, the grouping becomes stronger.

29
00:02:13.567 --> 00:02:18.187
One clue can be incomplete, so scientists compare independent evidence.

30
00:02:19.487 --> 00:02:25.247
Body structures, development, fossils, and DNA test the same branches from different

31
00:02:25.247 --> 00:02:29.167
angles. When they agree, the grouping gains support.

32
00:02:30.347 --> 00:02:32.287
The useful traits are derived traits.

33
00:02:33.467 --> 00:02:38.027
A derived trait first appeared in one ancestor and passed to its descendants.

34
00:02:39.027 --> 00:02:43.607
Trace shared derived traits to branch points to find the closest relatives.

35
00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:46.810
Now the first question has an answer.

36
00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:50.890
The whale's swimming shape links it to tuna by function.

37
00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:56.590
Its lungs, limb bones, and embryos link it to four-limbed land animals.

38
00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:01.010
Its milk, fossils, and DNA link it to mammals.

39
00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:06.410
Within living mammals, the evidence places whales closest to hippos.

40
00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:10.650
The tree records ancestry, not overall appearance.

41
00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:11.650
For more information visit www .fema .gov

42
00:03:11.833 --> 00:03:15.113
Looks still matter when they preserve inherited parts.

43
00:03:16.273 --> 00:03:19.793
They mislead when a shared job produces a similar form.

44
00:03:21.053 --> 00:03:25.953
So begin with traits, test them with independent evidence, and follow their paths

45
00:03:25.953 --> 00:03:27.313
toward shared ancestors.

46
00:03:28.653 --> 00:03:31.313
That turns a resemblance into a claim you can check.
